JDBC (Java Database Connectivity) - Way of connecting java application to Database.

                   To connect Database with java application we need
                   1. JDBC URL - jdbc:postgresql://localhost:8080/postgres -- Database name
                                         |              |       | -- Port
                                  Database type     Running on your own computer
                   2. DB User name
                   3. DB password
                   4. query

Syntax -
               Connection connection = DriverManager.getConnection(url , user, password)
               Statement statement = connection.createStatement();
               ResultSet resultSet = statement.executeQuery(query);

## DDL (Data Definition Language) - Defining/modifying the Structure of the Schema. Ex:- Create, Delete, Insert etc.
                                    For doing this we need to use executeUpdate(query) - this will not return anything.

## DML (Data Manipulation Language) - Work with the data inside the table. Ex - Select , Update, Alter etc.
                                      For doing this we need to use executeQuery(query) - this will return the data which can store in ResultSet.

## Row Mapper - Used to look the code cleaner and make the code reusable.

## Connection Pooling - Connection pooling in Java JDBC is a technique to reuse database connections instead of creating a new one for every request, which saves time and resources.
Popular libraries like HikariCP, Apache Commons DBCP, and C3P0 provide ready-to-use connection pools for PostgreSQL, MySQL, and other databases.

To connect database from Spring without wring any connection code mention all the connecting thing in application.yaml/yml or application.properties file and use JdbcTemplate.

There can be multiple databases and to make sure JdbcTemplate know which to connect we need to define it in yaml file by Primary and Secondary(defining properties of 2 DB)

Ex: -

Yml file has this
        datasource:
          primary:
            url: "jdbc:postgresql://localhost:8080/postgres"
            username: postgres
            password: 1101

          secondary:
            url: "jdbc:mysql://localhost:3306/mysqldb"
            username: admin
            password: admin

Need to create a class which define and tell JDBC to read the datasource and provide the name by @Qualifier

@Configuration
public class DatabaseConfig {

    @Bean(name = "primaryJdbcTemplate")
    public JdbcTemplate primaryJdbcTemplate(@Qualifier("primaryDataSource") DataSource ds) {
        return new JdbcTemplate(ds);
    }

    @Bean(name = "secondaryJdbcTemplate")
    public JdbcTemplate secondaryJdbcTemplate(@Qualifier("secondaryDataSource") DataSource ds) {
        return new JdbcTemplate(ds);
    }
}

Then where ever you use JbdcTemplate we need to annotate with @Qualifier("name of the bean").

Still we need to tell jdbc that @qualifier value need to connect with which Datasource(Primary or Secondary)

Ex-

@Configuration
@EnableTransactionManagement
@EnableJpaRepositories(
    basePackages = "com.example.primary.repository",   // package where your JPA repositories live
    entityManagerFactoryRef = "primaryEntityManagerFactory",
    transactionManagerRef = "primaryTransactionManager"
)
public class PrimaryDBConfig {

    @Bean(name = "primaryDataSource")
    @ConfigurationProperties(prefix = "spring.datasource.primary")
    @Primary - by mentioning this if someone does not annotate DataConfig methods by @Qualifier means it will use this one annotate by @Primary
    public DataSource primaryDataSource() {
        return DataSourceBuilder.create().build();
    }
}
